WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.590 --> 00:00:05.650
From tracksuits that have seen better days
to slick vinyl catsuits, we're here to investigate

00:00:05.650 --> 00:00:10.390
what DC's bad guys actually look like in the
comics they still call home, and just how

00:00:10.390 --> 00:00:13.180
different that is from what makes it onto
the silver screen.

00:00:14.180 --> 00:00:16.180
The Joker has a pretty packed wardrobe.

00:00:16.560 --> 00:00:17.560
Batman t-shirts?

00:00:17.960 --> 00:00:21.560
He's sporting them in Sean Murphy's miniseries,
Batman: White Knight.

00:00:22.620 --> 00:00:23.560
Sleeveless doctor's coat?

00:00:24.300 --> 00:00:26.020
Joker donned one in Batman: RIP.

00:00:26.020 --> 00:00:32.360
But still, over the decades, a central look
emerges: classic suits and tuxedos in green,

00:00:32.360 --> 00:00:33.520
orange, purple, and red.

00:00:34.260 --> 00:00:38.220
He first appeared in a large bowtie, purple
gloves, and well-pomaded hair.

00:00:38.840 --> 00:00:43.960
Though artists often add a couple unhinged
details to his look, most often mussed hair

00:00:43.960 --> 00:00:49.000
or outsize teeth, the Joker tends to keep
himself as well-turned-out as a Downton Abbey

00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:49.560
character.

00:00:50.260 --> 00:00:53.800
Modern movie audiences have three very different
Jokers to consider.

00:00:54.360 --> 00:00:59.220
Heath Ledger's clown is a mangy monstrosity
whose makeup visually flakes off throughout

00:00:59.220 --> 00:00:59.760
the movie.

00:01:00.500 --> 00:01:05.120
Joaquin Phoenix's take on the character looks
to famous mid-century television clowns like

00:01:05.140 --> 00:01:07.780
Bozo and Howdy Doody's Clarabell for inspiration.

00:01:08.520 --> 00:01:14.380
Jared Leto's demonic wild child swaps formal
wear for face tattoos and lurid purple coats

00:01:14.380 --> 00:01:15.360
over a bare chest.

00:01:15.930 --> 00:01:20.460
Each of these interpretations tells the audience
something different: Leto's Joker is more

00:01:20.469 --> 00:01:26.450
of a mafioso than a mastermind, Phoenix's
Joker is a 1970s everyman gone off the deep

00:01:26.450 --> 00:01:29.800
end, and Ledger's Joker is a nightmarish vision
of urban decay.

00:01:30.620 --> 00:01:34.040
But not one of them is as sharply dressed
as the madman of the monthlies.

00:01:35.000 --> 00:01:36.580
Some of those movie guys are just a mess.

00:01:37.420 --> 00:01:39.200
"Do I really look like a guy with a plan?"

00:01:40.220 --> 00:01:42.580
Doomsday's appeal is pretty much summed up
by his name.

00:01:42.880 --> 00:01:45.900
He is the reckoning at the end of Superman's
adventures.

00:01:46.540 --> 00:01:49.680
Appropriately, he is best known as the villain
of "The Death of Superman."

00:01:50.400 --> 00:01:55.860
The result of alien scientist Krypton's genetic
engineering, Doomsday is a grey goliath whose

00:01:55.860 --> 00:01:57.780
skeleton spikes through his skin.

00:01:58.240 --> 00:02:03.080
Doomsday's incarnation in Batman v Superman:
Dawn of Justice was a very different creature

00:02:03.080 --> 00:02:03.640
indeed.

00:02:04.400 --> 00:02:09.520
The product of Lex Luthor's meddling with
General Zod's corpse, this Doomsday was as

00:02:09.520 --> 00:02:13.500
large and gray as his comic counterpart, but
not quite as hard-edged.

00:02:14.120 --> 00:02:19.380
Though spikes of bone emerged mid-battle from
his skin, he starts out as a deformed grey

00:02:19.380 --> 00:02:19.920
brute.

00:02:20.700 --> 00:02:25.580
Moreover, this Doomsday developed the ability
to shoot blasts of energy out of his hands

00:02:25.620 --> 00:02:28.100
after absorbing the energy from a nuclear
strike.

00:02:28.920 --> 00:02:33.160
The Doomsday of the comics only develops this
ability after his fateful battle with the

00:02:33.300 --> 00:02:34.340
Outsiders Superteam.

00:02:34.880 --> 00:02:40.420
Doomsday on film is a grunting, primal vision
of brutality, something like the distant ancestor

00:02:40.500 --> 00:02:41.340
of the comics version.

00:02:42.540 --> 00:02:44.280
The feline fatale likes to keep it sleek.

00:02:44.850 --> 00:02:51.420
The Catwoman of the 1966 Batman TV series
accentuated basic black with glittering accessories

00:02:51.440 --> 00:02:52.300
and a striking mask.

00:02:53.380 --> 00:02:58.180
Batman Returns imagined Catwoman as a city
girl pushed to madness, symbolized by her

00:02:58.180 --> 00:03:00.560
visibly stitched patchwork of the catsuit.

00:03:01.380 --> 00:03:04.860
Halle Berry's costume was shredded, but still
as dark as the shadows.

00:03:05.590 --> 00:03:10.740
The Dark Knight Rises depicted Catwoman as
a burglar in matte black, with pointed stilettos

00:03:10.740 --> 00:03:12.400
and cat-eared tips on her goggles.

00:03:13.170 --> 00:03:17.920
These incarnations, though impressive in their
cinematic vision, rely on small details to

00:03:17.920 --> 00:03:19.800
do the heavy lifting of her characterization.

00:03:20.400 --> 00:03:25.520
But the Catwoman of comics has a more varied
wardrobe, ranging from emerald evening wear

00:03:25.600 --> 00:03:26.780
to royal purple catsuits.

00:03:27.760 --> 00:03:33.360
For all that, Trail of the Catwoman artist
Darwyn Cooke's 2001 redesign was so immediately

00:03:33.360 --> 00:03:33.920
iconic.

00:03:34.560 --> 00:03:40.240
In it, Catwoman wears a simple catsuit and
a cat-eared cowl, set off by flat-footed motorcycle

00:03:40.240 --> 00:03:41.460
boots and flared goggles.

00:03:42.380 --> 00:03:46.460
It's simple and remarkably practical, but
its sleek details make it timeless.

00:03:47.040 --> 00:03:50.740
Catwoman's taste might run to the extravagant,
but when she's on the job, she keeps things

00:03:50.740 --> 00:03:51.280
functional.

00:03:52.740 --> 00:03:53.760
"What are we gonna do!"

00:03:56.580 --> 00:04:00.820
"There's not much we can do, if that's who
I think it is."

00:04:01.420 --> 00:04:04.540
Being the literal god of war requires an impressive
outfit.

00:04:05.020 --> 00:04:08.080
Ares does just that in the 2017 Wonder Woman
film.

00:04:09.300 --> 00:04:13.960
With shining armor, spiked helmet, and enormous
weaponry, War sure ain't subtle.

00:04:14.560 --> 00:04:16.320
Ares of the comics follows suit.

00:04:16.820 --> 00:04:22.580
In George Perez's classic tale on Wonder Woman,
and Greg Rucka and Nicola Scott's recent revival,

00:04:22.580 --> 00:04:26.630
he wears dark blue armor, a plumed helmet,
and a billowing cape.

00:04:26.630 --> 00:04:31.200
It's largely the same design as what ended
up on screen, aside from one important difference.

00:04:31.860 --> 00:04:34.640
In the early comics, Ares has no discernible
body.

00:04:35.200 --> 00:04:38.240
Every inch of flesh is covered, save a narrow
gap in his helmet.

00:04:39.140 --> 00:04:43.140
On any normal human, this would reveal a portion
of the nose, lips, and chin.

00:04:43.720 --> 00:04:48.220
On the comic character, however, there is
only depthless darkness, studded with two

00:04:48.220 --> 00:04:49.220
smoldering eyes.

00:04:49.940 --> 00:04:55.420
Was it cool to see the man who played mild-mannered
Remus Lupin glowering beneath a helmet?

00:04:55.420 --> 00:04:56.020
Yes.

00:04:56.240 --> 00:05:00.100
But it would have been equally striking to
see his face disappear after doing away with

00:05:00.140 --> 00:05:01.020
his disguise.

00:05:02.080 --> 00:05:06.910
Remember when the villain of Suicide Squad
opened an apocalyptic portal through the art

00:05:06.910 --> 00:05:08.140
of aggressive belly dancing?

00:05:08.770 --> 00:05:14.400
In Enchantress' defense, when you're a millennia-spanning
interdimensional witch-goddess, you can pretty

00:05:14.420 --> 00:05:15.240
much make your own rules.

00:05:16.100 --> 00:05:20.750
That certainly applied to the character's
wardrobe, which shifted from rags and tarnished

00:05:20.750 --> 00:05:25.380
jewelry to a billowing two-piece affair, crowned
by a rune-inlaid headdress.

00:05:26.180 --> 00:05:28.570
Was it an outfit made for serious battle?

00:05:28.570 --> 00:05:29.120
No way.

00:05:29.500 --> 00:05:32.540
But Enchantress' powers allow her to do…other
things.

00:05:33.120 --> 00:05:34.320
"Please don't touch me.

00:05:34.920 --> 00:05:35.879
Please don't touch me."

00:05:35.880 --> 00:05:42.240
Introduced in a 1966 issue of Strange Adventures
as "The Switcheroo-Witcheroo," the Enchantress

00:05:42.240 --> 00:05:43.620
has cycled through many outfits.

00:05:44.500 --> 00:05:49.240
But all of them are in her signature bright
green and most feature a flamboyant cloak.

00:05:49.740 --> 00:05:54.379
Perhaps her most unique costume is her very
first: a classic witch hat, paired with a

00:05:54.380 --> 00:05:55.440
harlequin-patterned mini-dress.

00:05:56.380 --> 00:05:59.000
Not great for Suicide Squad, but perfect for
the swinging '60s.

00:06:00.180 --> 00:06:04.560
Doctor Poison is one of Wonder Woman's earliest
villains, and one of her most terrifying.

00:06:05.320 --> 00:06:10.160
A princess working as the head of the Nazis'
poison division, she disguises her gender

00:06:10.160 --> 00:06:13.700
under green doctor's gear and a mask with
a rictus grin.

00:06:14.610 --> 00:06:19.800
Her greatest creation was "Reverso," a poison
that would force Allied soldiers into doing

00:06:19.800 --> 00:06:21.160
the opposite of what they were told.

00:06:22.020 --> 00:06:26.900
Doctor Poison isn't a princess, in disguise
as a man, or working with the Nazis in 2017's

00:06:26.900 --> 00:06:27.440
Wonder Woman.

00:06:28.220 --> 00:06:29.640
But she is just as deadly.

00:06:30.240 --> 00:06:35.940
In an intriguing twist, Doctor Poison's mask
was reimagined as a series of flesh-colored

00:06:35.940 --> 00:06:40.540
plates, affixed to her face to hide the scars
exposure to her own creations wrought.

00:06:41.410 --> 00:06:46.180
Those plates aren't just striking, but rooted
in actual history: soldiers disfigured in

00:06:46.200 --> 00:06:48.780
World War I brought about new innovations
in prostheses.

00:06:49.720 --> 00:06:53.580
Doctor Poison might not have been the leering
specter of the Axis she began as, but she

00:06:53.580 --> 00:06:57.780
filled Wonder Woman audiences with a comparable
sense of terror.

00:06:58.720 --> 00:07:02.380
DC's New Gods exceeded conventional notions
of superheroes.

00:07:02.900 --> 00:07:08.080
They've got fabulous powers, extravagant outfits,
and wield technology capable of transporting

00:07:08.080 --> 00:07:09.120
them across dimensions.

00:07:09.870 --> 00:07:14.800
Steppenwolf is a New God, and his incarnation
in the Justice League lived up to that race's

00:07:14.820 --> 00:07:16.120
larger-than-life reputation.

00:07:17.160 --> 00:07:20.750
He was a CGI colossus of armor, stone, and
steel.

00:07:21.300 --> 00:07:26.800
Beneath his costume he was oddly waxen in
appearance, his skin a drained gray crisscrossed

00:07:26.800 --> 00:07:27.820
by alien grooves.

00:07:28.680 --> 00:07:33.510
Add in two bony outgrowths off his chin, and
you had a truly fearsome-looking opponent.

00:07:34.500 --> 00:07:35.500
"She's power.

00:07:37.400 --> 00:07:40.200
And power is the only law!"

00:07:41.700 --> 00:07:45.680
The Steppenwolf of modern comics is similarly
grand, but he didn't start out that way.

00:07:46.860 --> 00:07:52.120
He first appears in the New Gods series sporting
green medieval armor, complete with a bycocket

00:07:52.140 --> 00:07:55.430
hat, that makes him look more Robin Hood than
New God.

00:07:55.660 --> 00:08:01.120
But in modern renditions, his armor is red
and black, accented by a skull worn at his

00:08:01.120 --> 00:08:04.660
waist and red, claw-like stripes atop his
shoulders.

00:08:05.100 --> 00:08:09.750
In sharp contrast to the Steppenwolf of the
movies, however, his comic incarnation is

00:08:09.750 --> 00:08:10.420
notably handsome.

00:08:11.040 --> 00:08:13.740
He wears a dark beard, sometimes twisted into
long braids.

00:08:14.660 --> 00:08:20.360
Movie Steppenwolf is utterly unknowable, while
comic Steppenwolf is a man who, dressed differently,

00:08:20.360 --> 00:08:21.080
could pass for a hero.

00:08:22.150 --> 00:08:27.780
As a kid whose powers allow him to transform
into a buff, superheroic adult in a flashy

00:08:27.780 --> 00:08:32.240
red suit, Shazam embodies one of the most
potent fantasies in all of comics.

00:08:33.220 --> 00:08:37.140
Appropriately, his arch-enemy, Dr. Sivana,
is boldly drawn.

00:08:37.460 --> 00:08:41.920
Dr. Sivana, a criminal mastermind whose love
of depraved science is matched only by his

00:08:41.920 --> 00:08:42.600
ego.

00:08:43.080 --> 00:08:47.880
"Give me your power, or die."

00:08:48.540 --> 00:08:52.020
His appearance in Shazam! isn't too terribly
far off the mark.

00:08:52.580 --> 00:08:57.160
As in the comics, he is portrayed as a bald,
smirking man who oozes ill intentions.

00:08:57.960 --> 00:09:03.150
But unlike the villain Mark Strong portrayed
on screen, the Dr. Sivana of the comics is

00:09:03.150 --> 00:09:05.180
a hunched little man with an oversized head.

00:09:05.960 --> 00:09:11.100
His thick glasses obscure his eyes, which,
paired with the old-school lab coat he favors,

00:09:11.240 --> 00:09:14.220
create nothing so much as the very image of
the mad scientist.

00:09:15.300 --> 00:09:20.770
Add in the diabolical alien worm, Mr. Mind,
he is often seen with, and it all results

00:09:20.770 --> 00:09:23.380
in a man you'd give a wide berth to on the
street.

00:09:24.200 --> 00:09:28.160
Ra's al Ghul didn't become the head of the
League of Assassins through subtlety.

00:09:28.780 --> 00:09:33.230
He clawed his way to power over the course
of centuries, infiltrated the halls of the

00:09:33.230 --> 00:09:40.200
wealthy and connected through genius and determination,
and sired Talia al Ghul, herself one of Batman's

00:09:40.200 --> 00:09:41.100
most formidable foes.

00:09:41.830 --> 00:09:47.180
Accordingly, he cuts a fine figure: Ra's adorns
his roguish good looks with emerald green

00:09:47.180 --> 00:09:52.880
capes bordered in gold, intricately wrought
jewelry, and often a lush length of fur.

00:09:53.440 --> 00:09:59.060
Liam Neeson's portrayal in Batman Begins is
a dramatic departure from this rich refinement.

00:09:59.800 --> 00:10:04.340
There, Ra's blends into the shadows through
muted suits and dark training gear.

00:10:05.080 --> 00:10:09.520
He is less the distant master of a thousand
deadly hands and more the hands themselves,

00:10:09.520 --> 00:10:13.120
seeking to add Bruce Wayne to his ranks through
subterfuge.

00:10:14.080 --> 00:10:15.600
"You haven't beaten me.

00:10:16.000 --> 00:10:19.450
You have sacrificed sure footing for a killing
stroke."

00:10:20.220 --> 00:10:25.920
Though his subdued aesthetic is indeed appropriate
to that approach, one can't help but miss

00:10:25.920 --> 00:10:27.280
the flamboyance of his comic incarnation.

00:10:28.180 --> 00:10:30.900
The man who would become Ocean Master was
born to power.

00:10:31.560 --> 00:10:36.700
He's the type of guy who likes to refer to
his trident as a "scepter," and dresses accordingly.

00:10:37.400 --> 00:10:44.150
Orm wears purple scaled armor, a finned helmet
that covers his eyes with blank red oblongs,

00:10:44.150 --> 00:10:45.520
and a cape, even underwater.

00:10:45.970 --> 00:10:50.380
Ocean Master's final costume in Aquaman is
actually pretty darn close to what he wears

00:10:50.380 --> 00:10:51.800
in the comics, including the cape.

00:10:52.380 --> 00:10:56.760
But that resplendent outfit comes only at
the end of the film, during the final face-off,

00:10:56.760 --> 00:11:00.100
and is preceded by a host of other extravagant
costumes.

00:11:00.690 --> 00:11:05.160
He begins with dark armor of considerably
more bulk than the sleek looks favored by

00:11:05.160 --> 00:11:07.200
the other denizens of Atlantis.

00:11:07.660 --> 00:11:14.120
Later, he dons another set of armor wrought
entirely of gold, topped off by a finned crown.

00:11:14.120 --> 00:11:18.300
Orm's costumes send one very clear message:
he is to be obeyed, and he wants everyone

00:11:18.300 --> 00:11:18.840
to know it.

00:11:20.200 --> 00:11:22.880
Supervillain gimmicks range wildly in fearsomeness.

00:11:23.620 --> 00:11:27.520
Sometimes you get the Joker, essentially the
embodiment of chaos, and sometimes, well,

00:11:27.520 --> 00:11:29.740
you get a guy like Captain Boomerang.

00:11:30.460 --> 00:11:35.900
No one is exactly surprised to see a guy named
Captain Boomerang in a well-worn track suit

00:11:35.900 --> 00:11:40.920
and popped-collar trench coat in Suicide Squad,
missing a few teeth and sporting some facial

00:11:40.920 --> 00:11:41.520
scars.

00:11:42.080 --> 00:11:45.880
He's a guy who revels in being weird and even
a little off-putting, the kind of oddball

00:11:45.880 --> 00:11:49.720
who fits in right alongside Killer Croc and
Harley Quinn.

00:11:50.180 --> 00:11:53.740
Captain Boomerang is just as weird in the
comics, but with a good deal more panache.

00:11:54.460 --> 00:12:00.060
Rather than a ratty tracksuit, he began as
a man in a boomerang-patterned tunic, ascot,

00:12:00.060 --> 00:12:01.800
and jaunty blue garrison cap.

00:12:02.140 --> 00:12:06.690
Though recent incarnations have favored a
more toned-down look, his looks remain well-cut

00:12:06.690 --> 00:12:10.680
and elegant, and still often incorporate boomerang
patterns and an ascot.

00:12:11.360 --> 00:12:15.620
He might be strange, but he's going to look
good doing it, even when he's bothering his

00:12:15.620 --> 00:12:17.100
teammates with his boorishness and hijinks.

00:12:18.110 --> 00:12:23.140
One can only imagine the horror Captain Boomerang
of the comics would feel upon glimpsing his

00:12:23.140 --> 00:12:27.280
motley film self, to say nothing of the latter's
preoccupation with unicorns.

